  "strategy": "1. Approach and Rationale\nThe proposed solution introduces a deep reinforcement learning (DRL) framework with multi-layer architecture to optimize the function representation. The approach uses a combination of critic-action networks (CAN) and history-aware attention mechanisms to explore the complex solution space. Unlike previous methods that relied on local optimization and fixed representations, this approach:\n   - Dynamically balances exploration and exploitation through reinforcement learning\n   - Maintains awareness of function history to detect patterns and avoid local optima\n   - Incorporates multi-scale analysis to effectively minimize the overlap integral\n   - Uses Monte Carlo Tree Search (MCTS) to guide the optimization process\n\nKey improvements over previous strategies:\n   - End-to-end trainable framework\n   - Adaptive representation learning\n   - State-dependent optimization\n   - Theoretical grounding in optimal control theory\n\n2. Mathematical Specification\n- Representation: The function h(x) is represented as a continuous piecewise linear function \n  h(x) = \u03b8\u2099f(x - x\u2099) for n=1..N with base functions f\n- Decision variables: Function parameters and piecewise structure\n- Objective function:\n  min { max_k \u222b\u2080\u00b2 h(x)(1-h(x+k))dx }\n- Constraints:\n  \u222b\u2080\u00b9 h(x)dx = 1\n  0 \u2264 h(x) \u2264 1 \u2200x\n\nThe CORAL framework (Continuous Representation Attention Learning Architecture) is defined as:\n\u03a8(h, \u03b1) = E_{k\u223cP}[h(x)\u221a(1-h(x+k)) + \u03b1R(h)]\nwhere \u03b1 is regularization parameter, P is predefined kernel distribution\n\n3. Implementation Procedure\n- Phase 1: CNN-based base function generation\n  Inputs: [initial_h_values]\n  Operations:  \n    a) Normalize input using: \n        norm_h = (initial_h_values - min(initial_h_values))/(max(initial_h_values)-min(initial_h_values))\n    b) Apply 1D convolution with multiple kernels\n   \n- Phase 2: CAN network architecture\n  Modules:\n    i) Critic Network: \n        Architecture: (Conv1D)-[BatchNorm]-[ReLU]-[Dense]-[Sigmoid]\n        Objective: Value function approximation\n   \n    ii) Action Network:\n        Architecture: (Conv1D)-[BatchNorm]-[ReLU]-[Dense]-[Sigmoid]\n        Objective: Action probability distribution\n\n- Phase 3: Attention-enhanced optimization\n  Steps:\n    a) Compute multilevel correlation features\n    b) Apply history-aware attention mechanism\n    c) Execute MCTS self-play iterations\n\n- Phase 4: Progressive structure optimization\n  Technique: Kernel heredity preservation with pruning\n  Operations:\n    i) Compute fitness: f_value = min{compute_c5(h)}\n    ii) Propose candidate transformations\n    iii) Evaluate using Monte Carlo Tree Search\n\n4. Search Choices and Variations\n- Initial architecture variations:\n  a) Kernel width: 3-10 (default 5)\n  b) Layer depth: 3-7 (default 5)\n  c) Attention heads: 4-8 (default 6)\n\n- Optimization schedule:\n  Temperature schedule: t = t0 * exp(-epoch/\u03c4), \u03c4=500, t0=0.1\n  Exploration rate: \u03b5 = 1/k_explore, k_explore starts at 200\n\n- Core search variations:\n  i) Multi-scale sampling: uniformly spaced scales \u03b1 \u2208 [0.2,0.8] with probability 0.3\n  ii) Directional attention: \u03ba \u2208 [0.1,0.3] with probability 0.2\n  iii) Topology transition: q_trans \u2208 [0.4,0.6] with probability 0.1\n\n5. Compute Budget and Robustness\n- Staged execution:\n  1) Initialization: 20% budget\n  2) Attention learning: 30% budget\n  3) Structure optimization: 40% budget\n  4) Final refinement: 10% budget\n\n- Performance monitoring:\n  i) Wall-clock time check every 10th iteration\n  ii) Early stopping if no improvement of 1e-4 for 200 epochs\n  iii) Restart mechanism with parameter perturbation\n\n- Hardware utilization:\n  Primary: GPU acceleration for 75% computation\n  Backup: CPU implementation for 25% fallback\n\n6. Validation and Return Contract\n- Feasibility checks:\n  i) Verify 0 \u2